a.   The USDA Chief Information Officer (CIO) will:

       (1) Delegate executive leadership for geospatial technology, data, and information
           solutions to the USDA GIO;

       (2) Approve revisions to the USDA Geospatial Strategic Plan;

       (3) Review and approve policy waiver or exception requests. As the owner of this DR,
           the CIO will have the final review and approval authority for any waiver or
           exception requests;

       (4) Ensure appropriate procedural DMs are written to provide specific guidance for this
           DR; and

       (5) Approve DMs written to provide specific guidance to this DR.

  b.   The USDA GIO will:

       (1) Serve as the SAOGI, the senior executive responsible for Departmental geospatial
           strategy, planning, coordination, assessment, and synchronization of all USDA
           geospatial data activities, assigned to the GIO per OMB A-16 requirements;

       (2) Have authority, responsibility, and coordination for USDA’s geospatial information
           technology portfolio;




                                               6
 (3) Oversee and facilitate the USDA internal external and implementation of the
    geospatial IT and geospatial data policies, governance, directives, requirements,
    management, product, and service standardization;

(4) Review and ensure procedural DMs are written to provide specific guidance for this
    DR. These will be approved by the CIO prior to publication;

(5) Serve as the policy-level official representing USDA at the FGDC;

(6) Appoint Mission Area, agency, and staff office representatives to serve on FGDC
    coordination groups, governance, and working groups;

(7) Serve as GCCB Executive Champion for the activities of the GCCB;

(8) In collaboration with the GCCB, annually review the USDA Geospatial Strategic
    Plan, and update it at least every 5 years, as warranted;

(9) Maintain a knowledge base of USDA geospatial portfolio investments, oversee the
    enterprise geospatial capital planning process per DR 3130-013, Information
    Technology Capital Planning and Investment Control, and the investment control
    process per DR 3130-010, United States Department of Agriculture Enterprise
    Information Technology Governance, and advocate for Mission Area, agency, and
    staff office geospatial portfolio budgets and acquisition plans;

(10) Advocate for the use, value, and benefits of geospatial innovation and information
     supporting decision makers, citizens, partners, and others;

(11) Collaborate with the CIO, the EGMO Program Manager, the USDA Chief Data
     Officer, and other OCIO components to implement strategic initiatives;

(12) Provide the Departmental response to external geospatial portfolio data calls;

(13) Coordinate with the lead covered agencies for collection, acquisition, maintenance,
     and dissemination of NGDA data themes used by the USDA and coordinate and
     manage the annual lead covered agency reporting effort;

(14) Coordinate and manage the annual FGDC GDA Covered Agency Annual Report,
     and other reporting requirements and responses to the biannual covered agency OIG
     investigation;

(15) Review and provide a recommendation to the CIO on any submitted and completed
     waivers to the primary requesting office for any requirement that is not in
     compliance with this policy. The CIO, as the policy owner, will make the final
     decision;




                                        7
      (16) Implement and execute the necessary internal controls to proactively and regularly
          monitor, measure, audit, report, and enforce compliance with the provisions of this
          directive. This statement is also driven by DR 1110-002, Management’s
          Responsibility for Internal Control;

     (17) Delegate, as needed, others to act as the contact to coordinate with the lead covered
          agencies for collection, acquisition, maintenance, and dissemination of NGDA data
          themes used by the USDA and will coordinate and manage the annual lead covered
          agency reporting effort; and

     (18) Delegate, as needed, others who will coordinate and manage the annual GDA
          covered agency reporting requirements and responses to the biannual covered
          agency OIG investigation.

c.   The EGMO Program Manager will:

     (1) Act as the primary Departmental Program Manager for geospatial matters,
         overseeing and coordinating various programs, projects, and staff;

     (2) Manage enterprise-level geospatial software and services contracts as the
         Contracting Officer’s Representative;

     (3) Support and coordinate geospatial portfolio with Mission Areas, agencies, and staff
         offices;

     (4) Collaborate with USDA Mission Areas, agencies, and staff offices researching new
         geospatial technologies and potential implementations across Mission Areas,
         agencies, and staff offices to meet geospatial data management requirements;

     (5) Ensure that appropriate DMs are written to provide specific guidance for this DR.
         These will be reviewed by the GIO and approved by the CIO prior to publication;

     (6) Integrate geospatial data from all sources through collaboration, system
         architecture, infrastructure, and communications;

     (7) Serve as the GCCB Chair; and

     (8) Establish working groups to address geospatial data topics as needed for cross-
         agency coordination, including oversight for working groups arising from the
         GCCB.

d.   The USDA Chief Data Officer will collaborate with the CIO, the GIO, the EGMO
     Program Manager, and OCIO units on geospatial matters to implement strategic
     initiatives.




                                             8
 e.   USDA Mission Area, agency, and staff office Geospatial Leads will:

     (1) Serve as the members of the GCCB, to:

         (a) Review proposed changes to enhance, modernize, strengthen, or increase the
             impact of geospatial data, products, and services in USDA;

         (b) Provide expertise and perspective to support mission-focused products and
             services, modernization strategies, compliance with audit requirements, as well
             as statutory obligations under the GDA and other relevant USDA policies and
             regulations;

         (c) Consider methods to anticipate, monitor, and mitigate possible risks as it
             pertains to geospatial systems at the Departmental, Mission Area, agency,
             office staff levels;

         (d) Address goals and objectives described in the USDA Strategic Plan; and

         (e) Ensure prompt response to executive data calls, Federal mapping service
             requests, and information requests associated with FGDC reporting; and

     (2) Ensure the Mission Area, agency, or staff office geospatial guidance aligns with this
         DR and relevant DMs;

     (3) Be accountable for monitoring and reporting geospatial data quality, geospatial
         portfolio governance, metadata, and lifecycle management to ensure alignment with
         this DR;

     (4) Ensure that annual fiscal year geospatial investment portfolios meet business needs
         and geospatial guidance;

     (5) Complete the annual FGDC GDA Survey and provide feedback to the GIO for the
         FGDC GDA Covered Agency Annual Report and Self-Assessment;

     (6) Take responsibility to ensure that PII, CUI per DR 3440-003, and other legally
         restricted data is protected; and

     (7) Participate in determining whether declassified data can contribute to and become
         part of the NSDI per DR 3440-001, DR 3440-002, and DR 3440-003.

f.   USDA Mission Area, Agency, and Staff Office Heads will:

     (1) Align their policies and procedures with this DR within 6 months of its publication;

     (2) Develop and implement a 5-year road map, if they utilize or collect geospatial data,
         for geospatial program implementation within 1 year of DR publication;



                                            9
      (3) Ensure that geospatial data are managed in a coordinated and documented manner
         that meets the requirements of this DR;

     (4) Ensure a Geospatial Lead from their organization actively participates as a member
         of the GCCB;

     (5) Establish processes and infrastructure necessary to maintain, disseminate, preserve,
         and identify authoritative geospatial data;

     (6) Ensure that geospatial data management roles and functions are defined and
         assigned for their Mission Area, agency, or staff office, and GAD are understood
         and managed throughout the data lifecycle.

g.   The GCCB will:

     (1) Establish best practices for governance of geospatial investments and changes
         consistent with modernization and statutory guidance, and as informed by the GDA
         and OMB Circular A-16;

     (2) Be championed by the GIO, and chaired by the EGMO Program Manager;

     (3) Be comprised of Mission Area, agency, and staff office Geospatial Leads;

     (4) Address goals and objectives described in the USDA Strategic Plan;

     (5) Annually review and update the USDA Geospatial Strategic Plan, in collaboration
         with the GIO, and update it at least every 5 years, as warranted;

     (6) Review proposed actions to advance geospatial strategic initiatives;

     (7) Review proposed geospatial acquisitions;

     (8) Review proposed infrastructure or architecture changes consistent with USDA
         modernization objectives;

     (9) Consider methods to anticipate, monitor, and mitigate possible risks pertaining to
         geospatial systems at Mission Areas, agencies, and staff offices:

         (a) Including risks associated with data management;

         (b) Compliance with statutory obligations;

         (c) Computational and archival platforms (on premise and cloud); and




                                            10
            (d) Other factors that would inhibit the provision of mission critical geospatial
               products and services, including as described in USDA’s Geospatial Strategic
               Plan;

       (10) Use baseline Mission Area, agency, and staff office geospatial profiles (e.g., per
            regular surveys consistent with FGDC covered agency annual reporting templates)
            to inform proposed improvements, and to address the goals and objectives
            described in the USDA’s Geospatial Strategic Plan; and

       (11) Ensure prompt response to executive data calls, Federal mapping service requests,
            and information requests associated with FGDC reporting.
